questions.question:
Predict the output, then explain the fix.

for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) setTimeout(() => console.log("var", i), 0);
for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) setTimeout(() => console.log("let", j), 0);

questions.starter_code:
const createCounter = () => {
  // your code
};

const a = createCounter();
a.inc(); a.inc();
const b = createCounter();
b.inc();
console.log(a.value(), b.value(), a.count);

questions.answer:
const createCounter = () => {
  let count = 0;
  return {
    inc: () => ++count,
    reset: () => (count = 0),
    value: () => count,
  };
};

const a = createCounter();
a.inc(); a.inc();
const b = createCounter();
b.inc();
console.log(a.value(), b.value(), a.count);
// 2 1 undefined

count lives in the environment created by each call, and all three methods close over that same environment, so they share one variable per counter. a and b were created by separate calls, so they have separate environments. Because count is a closure variable and not a property, a.count is undefined — that is what makes it private, unlike a _count convention. Note reset returns the assignment's value; wrap it in braces if you prefer an explicit undefined.

questions.starter_code:
const memoize = (fn) => {
  // your code
};

let calls = 0;
const fib = memoize((n) => {
  calls++;
  return n < 2 ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
});
console.log(fib(10), calls);

questions.answer:
const memoize = (fn) => {
  const cache = new Map();
  return (n) => {
    if (cache.has(n)) return cache.get(n);
    const result = fn(n);
    cache.set(n, result);
    return result;
  };
};

let calls = 0;
const fib = memoize((n) => {
  calls++;
  return n < 2 ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
});
console.log(fib(10), calls);
// 55 11

The Map is created once per memoize call and captured by the returned function, so it persists between calls. Use cache.has before cache.get, because a cached 0 is falsy and `if (cache.get(n))` would miss it. The recursion must call the memoized fib; if the inner function called itself by name, it would bypass the wrapper and every subproblem would be recomputed — calls would jump from 11 to 177 for fib(10). The cache is unbounded, so for long-running processes cap the size or use an LRU.

questions.question:
What does this print, and what does it prove about closures?

function make() {
  let n = 0;
  const get = () => n;
  n = 5;
  return get;
}
console.log(make());

const user = { name: "Kakade", greet() { return () => this.name; } };
console.log(user.greet()());

questions.starter_code:
const debounce = (fn, delay) => {
  // your code
};

const search = debounce((q) => console.log("searching", q), 300);
search("ka"); search("kak"); search("kakade");

questions.answer:
const debounce = (fn, delay) => {
  let timer;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), delay);
  };
};

const search = debounce((q) => console.log("searching", q), 300);
search("ka"); search("kak"); search("kakade");
// after ~300ms of silence: searching kakade

If timer were declared inside the returned function, every call would get a fresh undefined, clearTimeout would do nothing, and all three calls would fire — that is throttling's behaviour, not debouncing. Declaring it in the outer function means all calls share one binding through the closure, so each call cancels the previous timer. The args are captured per call by the rest parameter, and the arrow inside setTimeout keeps them. Because the pending timer keeps the callback alive, cancel it on teardown if the component unmounts.
